+# Keep background jobs in the order database
+
+For whoever changes job scheduling, the worker or database operations. Background jobs live in Postgres alongside
+pantry orders, using procrastinate, after Redis evicted receipt jobs and members paid without receiving receipts.
+
+## Context
+
+In [issue 12](../../notes/issue-12.md), Nadia reported 300 missing receipts. The email provider had received nothing
+since Saturday at 23:10; the earlier provider bounce problem did not explain this incident. Tom found that the Redis
+instance holding Celery's queue had run out of memory and evicted keys, including queued receipt jobs. No error was
+logged when those jobs disappeared. Nadia resent the receipts by hand from the orders table, taking most of Tuesday.
+
+Redis was used only for the job queue, and Tom was the only volunteer who knew how to restore it. Postgres already
+held the orders, was backed up nightly and could be queried by every volunteer. The choice needed to prevent losing
+a receipt job between saving its pantry order and queuing the job, and reduce the services and specialist recovery
+knowledge the volunteers needed.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Keep Celery and Redis, add persistence and a memory alarm**: the least work that week; Tom also identified
+  sizing Redis for the worst week as necessary. Rejected because it retained a separate service used only for jobs
+  and dependence on one volunteer for recovery.
+- **Celery with its database backend**: proposed as a database alternative; rejected because Ana reported that
+  Celery's documentation marked it experimental.
+- **Keep jobs in Postgres with procrastinate**: chosen because it supports queuing a job in the same transaction
+  as its pantry order and uses the database the volunteers already operate.
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep background jobs in Postgres, using procrastinate, and enqueue a receipt job in the same transaction as the
+pantry order that queues it. This couples committing the order and its job: either both commit or neither does.
+Procrastinate was selected for this transaction support without retaining Redis or adopting the experimental Celery
+database option. Ana confirmed the choice on March 9 in issue 12.
+
+The [architecture](../architecture.md) locates the worker and describes the implementation available in this checkout.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Job storage shares Postgres operations and recovery knowledge with pantry orders. Redis is no longer needed for the
+queue; the migration discussion scheduled its shutdown after jobs moved to Postgres. Queue availability now depends
+on the same database as orders, and changing the order-writing or enqueueing code must preserve their shared
+transaction.
+
+Atomic enqueueing addresses the gap between storing an order and storing its job; it does not by itself establish
+that an email was delivered or that execution happens exactly once. Issue 12 records a manual resend from the orders
+table as the recovery used in that incident, but supplies no reusable resend procedure.
